1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,080
This counter misses timing at 250 MHz.

2
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:08,640
The worst path arrives 633 ps late.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:10,960
What would you change in the RTL?

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:14,060
Let's open the failing build in TimingLens,

5
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,440
try its suggestion and see what happens after
routing.

6
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:20,400
Start with a Vivado project or checkpoint,

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,660
or bring a saved timing capture.

8
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:25,460
Add the matching source and clock constraints.

9
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:28,740
Block designs and Tcl scripts can add context.

10
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:31,160
Here, choose design files.

11
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,020
We have the original capture,

12
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,120
the counter's SystemVerilog,

13
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:36,340
and its constraints.

14
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:39,000
Click Analyze selected files.

15
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:43,080
The overview shows 32 failing setup endpoints.

16
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,480
Click Review first issue.

17
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:49,380
TimingLens connects the measured path to a
specific question.

18
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:52,980
Can we shorten the comparison that drives the
register enable?

19
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:54,640
Open timing path.

20
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:59,400
Those four carry blocks sit between the counter
register and its enable.

21
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,620
The raw Vivado report puts a number on the
miss.

22
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:08,140
Worst setup slack is −0.633 nanoseconds.

23
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:11,960
Choose Review counter experiment.

24
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:13,700
Here's the suggested fix.

25
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:18,560
The original line compares a 32-bit count with
a 4-bit request.

26
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,900
In the replacement, any upper bit set means the
request fits.

27
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:26,160
Otherwise, compare just the low 4 bits.

28
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:29,220
The subtraction stays on the same clock edge.

29
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,080
Select Validate and run experiment.

30
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:39,180
First, both versions are checked against a
counter model over 10,289 simulation cycles.

31
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:44,080
Then, Vivado routes the original and the
candidate in separate copies,

32
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:48,520
using the same 4-nanosecond constraint and the
same Default recipe.

33
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,140
The implementation footage is sped up here.

34
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:53,540
Now, look at the Experiment Lab.

35
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:01,140
Worst setup slack went from −0.633 to +0.407
nanoseconds.

36
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:04,840
All 32 failing setup endpoints are gone.

37
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:07,320
Hold and pulse width pass too.

38
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:11,940
This standalone counter now meets its 250 MHz
target.

39
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:16,900
Export comparison saves both run records and an
HTML report.

40
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:22,680
Another engineer can review the exact RTL
change and the routed measurements behind it.

41
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:25,880
The next check is timing in the integrated
design.
